.TH RAMCTL 8
.SH NAME
ramctl \- configure RAM block devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ramctl create
.I special
.B size=value
[
.B backing=value
] [
.B compression
]
.br
.B ramctl destroy
.I special
.br
.B ramctl status
.I special
.br
.B ramctl list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ramctl
creates and removes runtime RAM block devices.  The
.B size
argument is the visible block-device size.  The
.B backing
argument is the physical RAM budget allocated when the command runs.  Values
accept K, M, and G suffixes.  If
.B backing
is omitted, it is equal to the visible size.  The visible size may use
.I Nx
to multiply an explicitly supplied backing size.
.PP
Without compression the visible size must equal the backing size.  With
.B compression
the visible size may exceed the physical backing, for example:
.PP
.RS
ramctl create /dev/ram1 backing=4M size=8M compression
.RE
.PP
The resulting ordinary block device may be formatted as a filesystem or as
swap.  It must be unmounted and removed from swap before it can be destroyed.
.PP
.B ramctl list
prints every currently configured RAM block device.  Unconfigured device
nodes are omitted.  Every available ramN device has the same capabilities;
the create arguments alone determine its backing, visible size, and whether
compression is enabled.  Destroying a device releases all of its backing and
compression-metadata pages to the VM allocator.
